# Everything the image build does not need. This is not only about speed: the build context is
# uploaded to whatever daemon or hosted builder performs the build, so anything listed here is
# something that would otherwise leave this machine.

# Build output. Six of these directories exist (target, target-cli, target-final, target-rent,
# target-sv2, target-verify) and together they are hundreds of megabytes of stale bytecode. The
# image builds its own `target/` from source inside the container, after the COPY, so excluding
# them here cannot affect it.
target/
target-*/

# Secrets. `.env.local` is what `run-local.ps1` loads and it holds real Cashfree keys; it is
# git-ignored, which is exactly why it needs naming here too — a .gitignore does not restrict a
# Docker build context. `.env.example` is a committed template and harmless, but excluding the whole
# family is the rule that stays correct when someone adds `.env.sandbox`.
.env
.env.*

# Key material, excluded by shape rather than by name: nothing here is expected to exist, and a deny
# rule that only lists remembered filenames fails on the one nobody thought of.
*.pem
*.p12
*.jks
*.key
key.json
*-key.json

# Local launchers. PowerShell scripts that set up a developer's machine; meaningless in a container
# and a source of stale hardcoded values if anyone ever ran one from inside the image.
run-local.ps1
run-lane-*.ps1
run-e2e-backend.ps1

# IDE and tooling.
.idea/
*.iml
.vscode/
tools/

# Deployment descriptors. `deploy/do-app-sandbox.yaml` is applied to DigitalOcean from CI, not read
# by the application, and shipping infrastructure topology inside the artefact it deploys is how a
# container ends up documenting its own attack surface to whoever gets a shell in it.
deploy/

# Logs and scratch.
*.log
logs/
